# Interview Transcript with Mohammad

**Q: Hello, can you tell me what your name is?**  
A: My name is Muhammad  

---

**Q: Who do you usually turn to for advice about course selection and why?**  
A: I’d say friends mostly people who have delved into the course profile deeper than I have or myself based on the course profile to see how easy and how viable it is. But the bottom line is mostly just my friends who know better, or myself.  

---

**Q: Would your confidence in course selection change if the advice came from someone with the same dual-degree background? Why or why not?**  
A: Not necessarily. It depends on the person. If this dual-degree person has the same subjects or major or bachelor program that I’m in, then I might. But the main factor isn’t the fact that they’re a dual-degree student but the fact whether they actually know the course well instead of being a double-degree student.

---

**Q: If so, how confident would you be on a scale of 1 to 10?**  
A: If the dual-degree student is not from the same program or major, probably a 3 or 4. But if they know the courses really well—the professors, assignments, assessments—and they’re also from the same major/program, then I’d say a 7 or 8.  

---

**Q: Do you think it would be helpful to your studies?**  
A: Assuming it’s the second case, the person knows a lot about the course and is willing to help.I guess yes. 

---

**Q: Do you think tailoring course options to your preferences, like avoiding finals or group work, would help you make course selections?**  
A: Yes. I usually look for courses with no exams, and I actually prefer doing group work.  

---

**Q: How much easier do you think it would be, on a scale of 1 to 10?**  
A: Ten.  

---

**Q: Tell me about a time when a course you wanted was full. How did it affect you?**  
A: There was a course called DECO3581, about medical. It affected me on emotional sense, because I wasn't fast enough. That was one of the courses that has no exam it mostly involved group work , I was really trying to do that one, but I ended up picking another course which had exams but the exams weren’t as hard as I expected. I felt disapointment with myself. I think that's how I felt.

---

**Q: How do you usually figure out alternatives when enrolment doesn’t go as planned?**  
A: I'd say maybe start with my friends who follows the same major/program. I would find other courses that probably has exam and I have to suck up but hoping the exam would be easy.

---

